WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
SwiftUI and React Native are built on similar structural assumptions,

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.960
meaning that the mental models you use in one often translate to the other.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.240
Despite relying on different underlying technologies,

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.480
both of these systems construct the exact same and result structures.

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.720
In both systems, the UI is declarative, component-oriented,

6
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.160
and updates automatically when the underlying state changes.

7
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.640
A common concern for iOS developers

8
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.200
is that moving to React means abandoning established native development patterns.

9
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.080
The shift is less about learning how to build a screen

10
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.240
and more about the specific way Swift and JavaScript handle data and functions.

11
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.240
Specifically, it requires moving from Swift's reliance on

12
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.320
structs and property wrappers to react use of closures and hooks.

13
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.680
By comparing SwiftUI patterns with React architecture,

14
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.440
we can see how the same UI logic applies to both environments.

15
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.520
Successfully moving between these platforms

16
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.960
is a process of translating the grammar of one into the other.

17
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.240
Every application starts with the same requirement,

18
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.240
rendering a static view.

19
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.280
This layout shows how a basic UI component is constructed in both ecosystems

20
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.360
with SwiftUI on the left and React TSX on the right.

21
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.400
The struct in SwiftUI corresponds to the function component in React.

22
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.400
Inside those structures, the computed body property in Swift

23
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.000
performs the same role as the return block in React.

24
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.680
Neither system uses imperative commands to mount the screen.

25
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.520
You simply describe the UI tree that the framework should render.

26
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.680
The next step is adding local state to make the component interactive.

27
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.920
SwiftUI uses the at-state wrapper as a local source of truth,

28
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.120
which is equivalent to the use-state hook in React.

29
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.440
In SwiftUI, you can change that state by assigning a value directly,

30
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.400
for example, by incrementing an account variable.

31
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.280
React uses a strict immutability pattern.

32
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.000
You never change the state variable directly.

33
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.280
Instead, you use the setter function returned by the hook.

34
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.760
This allows the React engine to detect the change and schedule a render.

35
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.880
While both approaches result in a reactive UI,

36
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.880
React enforces an explicit update pattern that SwiftUI often handles behind the scenes.

37
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.920
Applications usually require breaking the UI down

38
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.240
into nested child components.

39
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.720
Often, these child components must be able to update their parent state.

40
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.760
Here, we see the relationship between parent and child components in both languages.

41
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.760
SwiftUI facilitates this using the at-binding property record,

42
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.160
creating a two-way link where the child can modify the parent's data.

43
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.280
React does not have a direct equivalent to at-binding

44
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.400
and does not use implicit two-way data flow.

45
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Instead, it relies on a pattern called the controlled component.

46
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.600
The parent must explicitly pass the current state value down to the child as a prop.

47
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.560
The parent also passes a separate update function,

48
00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.440
an on-change callback, which the child calls whenever it needs to trigger a change.

49
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.440
Where at-binding allows a developer to treat a parent state

50
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.680
as if it were local to the child,

51
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.000
React requires that the data exchange remain explicit in the code.

52
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.880
This is known as unidirectional data flow.

53
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.640
React prioritizes this explicit traceable flow of data

54
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.680
over the automated wrappers found in SwiftUI.

55
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.240
As an app grows, it requires global state

56
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.200
that can be shared across the entire component tree.

57
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.000
This diagram represents injecting state from a central store into our components.

58
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.840
SwiftUI's at-observable object and at-published system

59
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.480
can be mapped to a zoo stand store in React

60
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.880
or use context for smaller scopes.

61
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.240
SwiftUI relies on classes and object identity.

62
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:47.440
ZooStan, by contrast, uses closures and hooks,

63
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.640
avoiding the need for decorators or class instances.

64
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.720
Moving to internal UI logic,

65
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.680
we also need to handle dynamic collections of data.

66
00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.080
The four-each iterator in SwiftUI

67
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.160
is functionally equivalent to the native.map method in JavaScript.

68
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.520
In SwiftUI, the ID parameter is used to track specific items in the list.

69
00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.400
In React, the key prop serves the same purpose,

70
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.520
helping the reconciler identify which items have changed

71
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.920
without rebuilding the entire list.

72
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.160
Finally, we must handle side effects,

73
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.200
such as running code when a specific value changes.

74
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.840
SwiftUI's dot-on-change modifier maps to the use-effect hook in React,

75
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.280
which uses a dependency array to determine when it should run.

76
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.280
If an operation needs to be canceled,

77
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.520
returning a cleanup function from use-effect

78
00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.200
performs the same role as calling cancel on a Swiftask.

79
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.600
React manages these life cycles and effects

80
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.360
through explicit dependencies,

81
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.080
rather than proprietary object life cycles.

82
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.400
The primary structural shift is simple.

83
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.120
Swiftstrucks are replaced by React functions.

84
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.000
State management moves from property wrappers to hooks,

85
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.680
and implicit bindings are replaced by explicit callbacks.

86
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.880
This table provides a quick reference for the mapping.

87
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.520
Body becomes a JSX return.

88
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.640
At environment object becomes use context,

89
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.680
and view modifier maps to style sheet.

90
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.280
These similarities extend to the actual development workflow.

91
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.360
The visual feedback loops in both environments are nearly identical.

92
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.320
The real-time updates provided by Xcode previews

93
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.720
are matched by expose fast refresh,

94
00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.240
which keeps the application state intact while you edit code.

95
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.040
Transitioning to React Native is essentially a process

96
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.640
of applying your existing UI logic using functional hooks

97
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.400
instead of property wrappers.

98
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.600
Because the underlying engineering logic remains consistent,

99
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.240
the transition from one to the other is a direct extension

100
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.240
of the skills you already have.

